.\" page (this project's own text), so it carries no third-party
.\" notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as cut.1/head.1.
.\" Rendered at build time to build/man/PASTE.TXT and staged on the
.\" play image as /usr/man/PASTE: `more /usr/man/PASTE`.
.TH PASTE 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
paste \- merge lines of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B paste
[
.B \-s
] [
.BI \-d " list"
]
.I file
...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Paste
merges lines from each named
.IR file .
At least one
.I file
is required; a lone
.B \-
in place of a file name means standard input.
.PP
Without
.BR \-s ,
each output line is the corresponding line of every
.I file
in turn, joined by the delimiter list (see
.B \-d
below) and terminated with one newline.  A
.I file
that has already run out of lines contributes an empty field
for the rest of the run \(em output continues until every
.I file
is exhausted, not merely the first one.
.PP
.B \-s
serializes instead: one output line
.I per file,
that file's own lines joined by the delimiter list.  An empty
file contributes no output line at all.
.PP
.BI \-d " list"
uses the characters of
.I list,
cycling and wrapping as needed, as the join delimiter instead
of the default
.BR <tab> .
The cycle restarts at the first character of
.I list
at the start of every output line.  The four escapes
.BR \e n ", " \e t ", " \e\e ", and " \e 0
stand for a newline, a tab, a literal backslash, and nothing at
all (not a NUL byte), respectively.  An empty
.I list
is an error.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/PASTE  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cut(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0.
Without
.BR \-s ,
every
.I file
must open before any output is produced; if one does not, a
diagnostic naming it is written to standard error, nothing is
written to standard output, and the exit status is 1.  With
.BR \-s ,
each
.I file
is opened and processed in turn; one that does not open is
skipped with a diagnostic, the remaining operands are still
processed, and the exit status is 1 if any operand failed to
open.  2 for a usage error, including no
.I file
operand at all, or a malformed
.B \-d
.IR list .
.SH BUGS
A line longer than 255 bytes is truncated at that length.  In
the default (parallel) mode, at most 5 files may be open at
once (this system's fixed 8-descriptor-per-process table, minus
the standard 3); a 6th operand is refused before any file is
opened.
